Skill	craft:github
Bash	git ls-files && cat billing/*.py accounts/*.py && git remote -v && gh auth status 2>&1 | head -5; ls; cat CLAUDE.md 2>/dev/null; git show --stat HEAD
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; cat $D/filing.md; echo ======; cat $D/tracker.md
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; sed -n '/full one it refuses/,/Ask the person/p' $D/filing.md | head -80; echo; echo "-----"; cat $D/tracker.md
Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; bash $D/scripts/collect.sh filing billing/tax.py accounts/signup.py vat sign_up email 2>&1 | tail -5; gh repo view acme/shop --json visibility --jq .visibility; gh label list
Bash	cat <tmp>/collect.MjTp0R
text	The tracker has no issue on these files yet. Next I file two new issues. Finding 1 is a nit, so it gets no issue.
Bash	T=$(mktemp -d) && cat > $T/vat.md <<'EOF'; ## Where; ; `billing/tax.py:4`: the docstring of `vat` says "Returns the VAT on amount, in cents."; ; ## How it shows up; ; `vat` returns `amount * 0.20`, quantized to `0.01`, as a `Decimal` in euros. Its only caller, `get_invoice_total` in `billing/invoice.py:5`, adds it to an order total in euros. A reader who trusts the docstring can divide the result by 100, or multiply the input by 100, and get a wrong invoice total.; ; ## Fix; ; Change the docstring to say that the result is in euros, as a `Decimal` rounded to the cent.; ; ## Why it was out of scope; ; Found in the review of the fix for #14, which changes only `orders/`. This file is in another module.; EOF; cat > $T/signup.md <<'EOF'; ## Where; ; `accounts/signup.py:4`: `sign_up` checks and stores the email as typed.; ; ## How it shows up; ; `sign_up("Bob@shop.test", h)` and then `sign_up("bob@shop.test", h)` both succeed. One person gets two accounts, and the check `if email in USERS` does not stop the second one.; ; ## Fix to decide; ; Normalize the email before the check and the store, for example with `email.strip().lower()`, and add a test for the case above. Decide if the stored value keeps the case as typed, and what happens to accounts that exist already with mixed case.; ; ## Why it was out of scope; ; Found in the review of the fix for #14, which changes only `orders/`. This file is in another module.; EOF; gh issue create --title 'The docstring of `vat` says cents, but it returns euros' --label documentation --body-file $T/vat.md; gh issue create --title 'sign_up treats emails that differ only in case as two accounts' --label bug --body-file $T/signup.md
text	I filed two of the findings as issues. I did not file the third, and I did not change any code. All three are outside `orders/`, which is the module of #14, so none of them goes into your branch.  1. **`get_invoice_total` → `invoice_total`** (`billing/invoice.py:4`): no issue filed. This is a naming preference, and no project rule asks for it. If you want to track it, file it yourself. 2. **`vat` docstring says cents** (`billing/tax.py:4`): filed as [#17](https://github.com/acme/shop/issues/17), labelled `documentation`. A docstring that says something false gets an issue, because a reader who trusts it can calculate a wrong invoice total. 3. **Emails that differ only in case** (`accounts/signup.py:4`): filed as [#18](https://github.com/acme/shop/issues/18), labelled `bug`. The issue asks you to decide two things: does the stored email keep the case as typed, and what happens to existing accounts with mixed case.  Before filing, I searched the tracker for these files and names. No open or closed issue covers them. The repository is public, and the issues contain only code from this repository.  I did not touch the branch or the pull request. Because I changed no code, I did not run a check, and no docs needed an update.
